{"html":"<p>The great city on the Bosporus, capital of the <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"eastern-roman-byzantine-empire\">Eastern Roman (Byzantine) Empire</a> for over a thousand years and then of the <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"ottoman-empire\">Ottoman Empire</a>. Today it is Istanbul.</p><p>It began as the Greek colony of <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"byzantium-founded\">Byzantium</a>. In 330 <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"constantine-the-great\">Constantine the Great</a> refounded it as a new Rome and his capital. The land walls built under Theodosius II in the 400s held off sieges for centuries, and <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"hagia-sophia\">Hagia Sophia</a> rose under Justinian in 537. For most of the Middle Ages it was the largest and richest city in Europe.</p><p>Its site made it a hub of trade. Ships from the Black Sea had to pass it on the way to the Mediterranean, and goods from Asia crossed to Europe here. The Rus came down the rivers to trade and to serve in the emperor's guard. Italian merchants from Venice and Genoa won quarters of their own, such as the <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"genoese-colony-at-galata\">Genoese colony at Galata</a>.</p><p>Two falls mark its later story:</p><ul><li>In 1204 the army of the Fourth Crusade <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"crusaders-sack-constantinople\">sacked the city</a> and set up a Latin empire. The Byzantines <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"constantinople-retaken\">took it back</a> in 1261, but it never recovered its wealth.</li><li>In 1453 <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"mehmed-ii\">Mehmed II</a> took it with cannon. The <a class=\"kbl\" data-k=\"fall-of-constantinople\">fall of Constantinople</a> ended the Byzantine Empire, and the city became the Ottoman capital until 1923.</li></ul>","links":["eastern-roman-byzantine-empire","ottoman-empire","byzantium-founded","constantine-the-great","hagia-sophia","genoese-colony-at-galata","crusaders-sack-constantinople","constantinople-retaken","mehmed-ii","fall-of-constantinople"],"sum":"The great city on th